From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 00:21:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 32/33] fix(gastown): stop the reaper reporting its own budget
 as a data-plane failure (gcp-mqu9)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

polecat-worktree-reap.sh bounds every external call by the time left in its
own budget, and run_bounded returns 124 for two very different things: the
command ran and overran, or the budget was already spent so the command NEVER
RAN. Callers could not tell those apart and described both with wording that
named a specific external cause — `git status failed in the worktree`, `bulk gc
bd show returned no usable JSON`. Neither is true in the truncation case: git
status did not fail, it never ran; the bulk query returned nothing because it
was never issued.

That is not a cosmetic complaint. It produced two wrong diagnoses in one night,
both aimed at a Dolt server that was healthy throughout, and gcp-3ty was
labelled `worktree_status_unreadable` with a worktree that is perfectly clean.
A silent diagnostic costs time; one that names the wrong subsystem costs more,
because people believe it.

Every bounded call site now classifies its result (classify_outcome: skipped /
timeout / failed / ok) and reports what actually happened. A check the budget
never allowed is a new `worktree_budget_truncated` event whose reason names the
check that did not run and nothing external. A check that ran and overran keeps
its old event but says so, naming the seconds it was given out of the budget —
so a reader can see at a glance whether the endpoint is slow or the cycle was
short. Only a genuine non-zero exit is now reported as a failure of the thing
called. classify_outcome takes the limit as an argument rather than reading
state run_bounded sets, because the git-status call site captures stdout in a
command substitution and anything the function set about itself would die with
that subshell.

Log lines are now stamped when the event happens. Every line used to carry the
RUN's start time, so a cycle that spent its whole eight-second budget was
indistinguishable from an instant one and the ordering of a slow cycle's
decisions was unrecoverable. The run stamp is kept as its own `run_started`
field so lines can still be grouped into cycles, and every line carries
`budget_remaining` — a line reading 0 is self-evidently the reaper's clock
rather than the subsystem the event names. The budget-exhausted and
budget-truncated details carry examined/skipped/deferred counts, matching what
the explicit truncation event already did well.

Also fixed the quietest instance of the same defect: when the budget expired
before `git worktree list`, the run printed "no per-bead polecat worktrees under
<rig>" — a definite claim about a rig it had not looked at — and logged nothing
at all. It now records the truncation (or a real `worktree_list_failed`) and
says examined=0.

The mol-witness-patrol legend told the reader that a repeating
`worktree_owner_unconfirmed` means the rig's Dolt endpoint is the problem "not
the reaper", which is exactly backwards for the truncation case. The log-review
table now documents the reason values, points at the budget first for every
timed-out variant, and reserves the endpoint reading for genuine failures.

Validation: `bash gastown/tests/test_polecat_worktree_reap.sh` — three new tests
cycled red against the pre-fix script and green after, each failing for its own
defect (one distinct ts across a 2s run; "no per-bead polecat worktrees" with
the list unread; a real git-status failure indistinguishable from a short
budget). They drive the classification end to end through a git stub that can
stall or fail the reaper's own git calls. test_gastown_pack_assets.sh gains a
guard that every event the reaper can record has an entry in the patrol legend,
so the two cannot drift again. Full gastown shell suite green; `go vet ./...`,
`golangci-lint run ./...`, `validate_registry.py`, `go test -race ./...` green.

Note: the gates themselves are untouched and deliberately so — an unreadable
bead, an unconfirmable owner, and an expired budget all still skip the reap.
Only the reporting changed.

Follow-up: gcp-2vwn (pre-existing `tests/test_no_bare_bd_commands.py` failure on
integration, in two gastown test stubs this change does not touch).
